{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 6,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.732,
            0.064,
            0.834,
            0.081
          ],
          "content": "Prime Time"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.121,
            0.834,
            0.165
          ],
          "content": "What about 1, which has only one factor? The number 1 is neither a prime nor a composite number."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.174,
            0.833,
            0.218
          ],
          "content": "How many prime numbers are there from 21 to 30? How many composite numbers are there from 21 to 30?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.227,
            0.663,
            0.246
          ],
          "content": "**Can we list all the prime numbers from 1 to 100?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.249,
            0.833,
            0.295
          ],
          "content": "Here is an interesting way to find prime numbers. Just follow the steps given below and see what happens."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.303,
            0.765,
            0.324
          ],
          "content": "**Step 1:** Cross out 1 because it is neither prime nor composite."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.326,
            0.431,
            0.391
          ],
          "content": "**Step 2:** Circle 2, and then cross out all multiples of 2 after that, i.e., 4, 6, 8, and so on."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.394,
            0.431,
            0.506
          ],
          "content": "**Step 3:** You will find that the next uncrossed number is 3. Circle 3 and then cross out all the multiples of 3 after that, i.e., 6, 9, 12, and so on."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.508,
            0.431,
            0.619
          ],
          "content": "**Step 4:** The next uncrossed number is 5. Circle 5 and then cross out all the multiples of 5 after that, i.e., 10, 15, 20, and so on."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.445,
            0.329,
            0.831,
            0.586
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.621,
            0.832,
            0.665
          ],
          "content": "**Step 5:** Continue this process till all the numbers in the list are either circled or crossed out."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.674,
            0.548,
            0.763
          ],
          "content": "All the circled numbers are prime numbers. All the crossed out numbers, other than 1, are composite numbers. This method is called the Sieve of Eratosthenes."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.766,
            0.548,
            0.879
          ],
          "content": "This procedure can be carried on for numbers greater than 100 also. Eratosthenes was a Greek mathematician who lived around 2200 years ago and developed this method of listing primes."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.611,
            0.673,
            0.794,
            0.725
          ],
          "content": "It is definitely not some magic; there should be a reason why it works."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.567,
            0.65,
            0.831,
            0.883
          ],
          "content": "",
